The disassembler fold is real, guest-invisible, and already shown by the witness census (`ld r5,-95(r21)`, `ld r6,10(r22)`). File it at low severity with that witness. No new run. Out of this round, as Q1(g).

`ldx`, `ldux`, `lwax`, and `lwaux` are real and out of scope. READ: `opcodes_ppc.h` has `PPC_31_STDX` (149) and `PPC_31_STDUX` (181), and the indexed arm implements them with no width test. There is no define for XO 21, 53, 341, or 373, so those words hit opcode 31's `default: goto bad` on every CPU, including PPC970. A 64-bit guest's indexed doubleword load or algebraic word load stops the emulator; the matching store is implemented. No hit for those names in `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`. I did not open `ledger.json`. R17: different site, not witnessed here, and the oracle would be an indexed EA. Its own round if it is filed. The class is an unimplemented instruction (CPU stop), not a misdirected store.

Also guest-invisible and not this round: the disassembler has no case for `LFDU`, `LFSU`, `STFDU`, or `STFSU` (no matches in `cpu_ppc.c`). Those opcodes execute since `#310` and disassemble as an unimplemented primary opcode.


