 769: ## 6. PREMISES STATED AS QUESTIONS -- check these; ratification is not wanted
 770: 
 771: * **P1 -- ENUMERATE the repaired displacement.**  `imm = (int16_t)(iword & ~3)` clears the word's two lowest bits (XO) and
 772:   sign-extends the low halfword.  I claim it equals EXTS(DS || 0b00) for every DS and every XO, because the clear never
 773:   touches the halfword's sign bit.  Walk DS = -8192, -1, 0, +1, +8191 against XO 0..3 (twenty cells); does any differ?
 774: * **P2 -- the two uses of `imm`.**  The arm uses `imm` in the table index (`imm==0? 16 : 0`, the `_0` family) and as
 775:   `ic->arg[2]`.  I read every `_0` handler on a 64-bit CPU as handing the access to the GENERIC handler, which ADDS
 776:   `(int32_t)ic->arg[2]` anyway (`LS_GENERIC_N` is compiled only under `#ifndef LS_IGNOREOFS`, so a `_0` pass reuses the
 777:   generic function its non-`_0` pass defined; 8.7, 8.8).  If so, on PPC970 the `_0` half is unobservable by value, the
 778:   result rests on `ic->arg[2]` alone, and the two uses matter only for DS = 0 words whose XO is non-zero.  Check; what
 779:   does it imply for rows at DS = 0?
 780: * **P3 -- the slots.**  `ld` -> 15 / 31 (`_0`); `ldu` -> 47 / 63; `lwa` -> 10 / 26 (`size` 2, `zero` 0 -- `ppc_instr_lwa`,
 781:   which casts the word through `(int32_t)`); `std` -> 7 / 23 and `stdu` -> 39 / 55 (the arm leaves `zero` 1 for stores;
 782:   slots 3 / 19 / 35 / 51 hold the same handlers).  Check against 8.8; can any flag combination the design plausibly
 783:   produces for these forms land on 11 / 27 / 43 / 59 (`ppc_instr_invalid`)?
 784: * **P4 -- update order** (S8).  `stdu` with RA = RS stores RS's OLD value and then writes RA <- EA (Book I: valid); `ldu`
 785:   with RA = RT writes RT with the doubleword and then RA (= RT) <- EA, so RT ends holding EA.  Check.
 786: * **P5 -- RA = 0 after the repair.**  The arm's existing guard -- `if (ra == 0 && update) { ... fatal("TODO: ra=0 &&
 787:   update?\n"); goto bad; }` after the table selection (the D/DS-form arm's copy; the indexed arm has a second) -- reaches
 788:   `goto bad` for `ldu`/`stdu` with RA = 0 once the repair sets `update`.  Today those words execute as `ld`/`std` from the
 789:   ABSOLUTE address (DS || XO), no update.  So the repair turns "executes at an absolute EA+1" into "the CPU stops".  Check;
 790:   is that change right?
 791: * **P6 -- no illegal-instruction delivery exists** (S5, S6): the only disposition for an illegal PowerPC encoding is `goto
 792:   bad`.  Did I miss a Program-interrupt path (grep `ppc_exception(`, `0x700`, `PROGRAM` under `src/cpus/`)?
 793: * **P7 -- the 32-bit sibling census** (S12).  One 64-bit-only family is gated on 32-bit CPUs (`rld*`), one is not
 794:   (`stdx`/`stdux`), one is absent everywhere (`ldx`/`ldux`/`lwax`/`lwaux`).  Complete?  Is any other 64-bit-only
 795:   instruction in `X(to_be_translated)` gated or ungated (`mulld`, `divd`, `sld`, `srd`, `srad`, `extsw`, `cntlzd`, `td`,
 796:   `ldarx`/`stdcx.` ...)?
 797: * **P8 -- the EA domain after the repair.**  No alignment gate exists on this path: no alignment vector (S5); the fast path
 798:   hands every unaligned non-byte access to the generic handler (`if (addr & (LS_SIZE-1)) { LS_GENERIC_N(cpu, ic); return;
 799:   }`); on PPC970 every access is generic anyway (S8).  Book III v2.01's Alignment-interrupt list (8.11) names no big-endian
 800:   single-register fixed-point load or store; the PEM (:11975) says a misaligned access "may either cause an alignment
 801:   exception or ... break the memory access into multiple, smaller accesses".  So after the repair the EA can be ANY byte
 802:   address -- a base that is not a multiple of 8 gives an unaligned `ld`/`std` -- as for `lwz` today.  Check; does
 803:   anything in the design assume a 4- or 8-aligned EA?
 804: * **P9 -- the predicted pre-fix stores** (section 2): `std` correct; `stdu` at EA+1, RA unchanged; 62/XO 2 and XO 3 at EA+2
 805:   and EA+3.  Check against 8.2 and 8.7.
 806: * **P10 -- the disassembler shares the fold, and is guest-invisible.**  Its load/store arm takes `imm = (int16_t)(iword &
 807:   0xffff);` and prints `ld` for every opcode-58 word and `std` for every opcode-62 word, with no XO test (8.9); the
 808:   witness's `unassemble` shows `ld r5,-95(r21)` for an `ldu` word and `ld r6,10(r22)` for an `lwa` word.  The debugger's
 809:   `unassemble` calls it with `running = 0` (`len = cpu_disassemble_instr(m, c, buf, 0, addr);`,
 810:   `src/debugger/debugger_cmds.c`), which reads nothing; the instruction trace calls it with `running = 1`, and for loads it
 811:   previews a `NO_EXCEPTIONS` read of the computed address (the witness's `<0x20021: ...>`).  Neither writes guest state.
 812:   Check both claims.
 813: * **P11 -- the witness's validity (R14).**  (i) RED on HEAD shown; the post-fix GREEN half owed.  (ii) Liveness = `live`;
 814:   the ladder's "device-signature" control is written for device access -- here its role (the value could only have come
 815:   from the decode under test) is played by the counting pattern, which names the offset, beside the green `ld` (XO 0) and
 816:   `lwz` controls on the same pattern.  (iii) Unmodified `bareppc`, no CPU override.  Sufficient for a DECODE witness, or is
 817:   a control missing?
 818: * **P12 -- `testppc` versus `bareppc`.**  The design names `testppc`; the witness used `bareppc`.  Both are `machine_test.c`
 819:   entries whose `MACHINE_DEFAULT_CPU` sets `machine->cpu_name = strdup("PPC970");`; `bareppc` has no devices.  Equivalent
 820:   for every row this round owes?
 821: * **P13 -- clause (b)'s fixed sites.**  Three edited decoder lines -- the displacement line and the two XO-blind `case`
 822:   lines -- plus the disassembler's displacement and two mnemonic lines if Q1(g) joins it.  Any other site?
 823: * **P14 -- the D-form control cannot see the repair's scope.**  The design clears the low two bits for opcodes 58 and 62
 824:   ONLY.  The witness's `lwz` control has displacement +8 (low bits 00), so it reads the same whether the clear reaches
 825:   58/62 only or every opcode of the arm.  Check; Q2(e) asks what row that owes.
 826: * **P15 -- a `goto bad` word ends its session's execution.**  `bad:` sets `cpu->running = 0`, so the words after it in a
 827:   stepped session do not run.  The shipped `ppc_rldicl_probe.py` puts its 32-bit `goto bad` word LAST in its own session,
 828:   after a liveness `li` (9.5: rows `bits32 live`, `bits32 rld bad`).  So each illegal-XO word, and each RA = 0 update
 829:   word after the repair, needs its own session or the last slot of one.  Check.
 830: 
