Read-only. No build, no run. Line numbers below match `GXEMUL-SEC/src/` (the arm is at 4277/4288/4307/4320). `S12` is incomplete. `P2`'s "only DS=0" clause is the wrong place to spend a row.

## Q1

**(a) Five forms, P1, P3.** READ. `tmp_ppc_loadstore.c:893-957` is a flat 64-entry table. Index = `size + 4*zero + 8*load + 16*(imm==0) + 32*update`. The arm's initial flags are `load=0, zero=1, size=0, update=0`.

| form | flags | slot | slot if imm==0 | handler |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `ld` | load, size 3, zero 1 | 15 | 31 | `ppc_instr_ld` / `ld_0` |
| `ldu` | those + update | 47 | 63 | `ldu` / `ldu_0` |
| `lwa` | load, size 2, zero 0 | 10 | 26 | `lwa` / `lwa_0` |
| `std` | size 3, zero 1 | 7 | 23 | `std` / `std_0` |
| `stdu` | those + update | 39 | 55 | `stdu` / `stdu_0` |

Slots 11, 27, 43, 59 are `ppc_instr_invalid` (`fatal` + `exit(1)`). They are exactly `load && !zero && size==3`. The design's five flag combinations do not land there. No handler edit. The nearby miss that does host-exit is `lwa` coded as size 3 with `zero` cleared. `lwa` must stay size 2, `zero` 0. The sign-extend is the existing `(int32_t)` cast at `cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c:119-120` (READ).

P1, enumerated. Producer of `(DS, XO)` is the low halfword: DS is 14-bit signed, XO is bits 1:0, so all 65536 patterns, not a sample the decoder invents. `imm = (int16_t)(low & ~3)` against `EXTS(DS || 0b00)`:

| DS | XO | low half | masked int16 | EXTS(DS\|\|00) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| -8192 | 0..3 | 0x8000..0x8003 | -32768 | -32768 |
| -1 | 0..3 | 0xFFFC..0xFFFF | -4 | -4 |
| 0 | 0..3 | 0x0000..0x0003 | 0 | 0 |
| +1 | 0..3 | 0x0004..0x0007 | 4 | 4 |
| +8191 | 0..3 | 0x7FFC..0x7FFF | 32764 | 32764 |

No cell differs. Clearing bits 1:0 never touches the int16 sign bit (bit 15), so the other 65516 halfwords match for the same reason. `(int16_t)low & ~3` matches those twenty as well. `iword` is `uint32_t` and `int` is 32-bit on this host, so `~3` converts to `0xFFFFFFFC` before the AND (READ, types at `cpu_ppc_instr.c:4074-4078` and the assignment at 4295).

**(b) P2.** READ. `ppc_instr_ld_0` is built with `LS_IGNOREOFS` and `LS_GENERIC_N ppc_generic_ldz` (`tmp_ppc_loadstore.c:270-277`). The generic body is compiled only on the non-`_0` pass (`#ifndef LS_IGNOREOFS`). On PPC970, `LS_N` hits `if (!cpu->is_32bit) { LS_GENERIC_N; return; }` (`cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c:192-198`) before the fast path can ignore the offset. `cpu->is_32bit` is `(bits == 32)` (`cpu_ppc.c:120`), and PPC970 is the 64-bit implementation. Both `_0` and non-`_0` add `(int32_t)ic->arg[2]`. The `_0` half is unobservable by value on this CPU.

The two uses can see different numbers only when XO is nonzero. When XO is 0 the mask changes nothing, at every DS. The guest-visible use is `ic->arg[2]`. Leaving that use raw is wrong at every DS, including nonzero DS. Leaving only the `imm==0` index raw is invisible on PPC970, including at DS=0, because both handlers add `arg[2]`. P2's "the two uses matter only for DS=0" is the inverted conclusion: DS=0 is where the index branch flips and where that flip cannot be seen.

The design is safe only if one masked `imm` feeds both the index at 4335 and `ic->arg[2]` at 4351. The record has to say that, because the current assignment at 4295 sits before both and is shared with the other twenty-two opcodes. A second variable that only one of the two sites reads does not implement the design.

**(c) Illegal XO.** READ. `ppc_exception(` under `src/` is DEC, EI, FPU, SC, and the storage interrupts in `memory_ppc.c`. `cpu_ppc.h:252-257` has no Program (0x700) and no Alignment (0x600). P6 stands. Member 5 (FAULT) is not available. Member 7 would execute the reserved XO, which is the defect. `goto bad` is the disposition this function already uses: `fatal()` prints and returns (`debugmsg.c:384-390`, it does not exit), then `bad:` sets `cpu->running = 0` (`cpu_dyntrans.c:2014`). No latch. The CPU stops on the first one, so it cannot recur.

Print a `fatal("TODO: ...")` before `goto bad`, outside read-ahead, same shape as the RA=0 guard at 4338-4341 and `rld*` at 4602-4605. Under `step`, `bad:` skips `DISASSEMBLE` (`cpu_dyntrans.c:2011-2012`, the `single_step` test). A row that keys off the disassembly line inside `bad:` fails on a stepped session. The `rldicl` probe keys off the `fatal` text for this reason.

**(d) D1, P4, P5.** READ. Store path copies RS into a local `uint64_t` and then calls `memory_rw`; the update write is after a successful store (`cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c:163-184`). `stdu` with RA=RS stores the old value and then writes EA. Valid. Nothing in the design treats it as invalid.

`ldu` with RA=RT writes the doubleword through `arg[0]`, then writes EA through `arg[1]`. Those pointers are the same GPR, so RT ends as EA and the loaded value is discarded. Book I allows the illegal-instruction handler or boundedly undefined results for an invalid form. CRIT is the merged ruling: disclosed, not checked. Astra's "rather than executing them as plain `ld`/`std`" is what reserved XO and today's update-less decode do. RA=0 becomes distinguished by the existing guard once `update` is set. RA=RT does not. The sibling update loads in this same arm (`lbzu`, `lhzu`, `lhau`, `lwzu`) do not test RA=RT either. Adding a check only for `ldu` is a new predicate, and the only refusal on hand is a CPU stop. Leave it disclosed. The contract states the post-repair result: RT holds EA.

P5 is right, and the change is the one this arm already makes for every other update form. Today opcode 58/62 leaves `update` at 0, so RA=0 takes the zero register (`cpu_ppc.zero`, allocated by `zeroed_alloc` in `cpu.c:71`) plus the raw halfword and does not stop. After `update` is set, 4338-4341 stops the CPU. That replaces a guest-visible absolute access at EA+1 with the tree's illegal-instruction stop. Book I's invalid-form rule allows that.

**(e) 32-bit.** Disclose, and write the delta in the contract. Do not add the width test in this round.

S9 is true: one source builds both decoders, and an edit of the arm changes both. PEM :6253 makes 58 and 62 illegal on a 32-bit implementation (DERIVED from the inlined manual). The `rld*` idiom would transfer (R13). It still does not join this round. The filed defect is the XO fold on the 64-bit CPU. A width gate is a different claim (illegal opcode versus wrong form), it is not witnessed, and R17 keeps `stdx`/`stdux` where they are. P7, READ: S12 is not the whole census. Gated on `bits==32`: `rld*` only. Implemented with no width test: `stdx`, `stdux`, `sld`, `extsw`, `ldarx`, `stdcx.`. No `PPC_31_*` define, so `default: goto bad` on every CPU: `ldx`, `ldux`, `lwax`, `lwaux`, `mulld`, `divd`, `srd`, `srad`, `cntlzd`, `td`. Primary opcode 2 has no case. Gating 58/62 alone would be a third policy in the same function, next to families that still execute.

The contract has to say the consequence, or the delta is silent: after the repair a 32-bit CPU executes `ldu`, `lwa`, and `stdu` as the 64-bit forms. Today it executes every XO as an 8-byte `ld`/`std` with the fold.

**(f) P8.** READ. No alignment vector is raised. Book III :3794-3814 lists FP misalignment, `lmw`/`stmw`/`lwarx`/`ldarx`/`stwcx.`/`stdcx.`/`eciwx`/`ecowx`, little-endian single-register accesses, and the string/multiple cache cases. Big-endian `ld`/`std` are not in that list. PEM :11975 allows a split. On PPC970 the handler is the generic path, which has no alignment gate. Grok's "8-aligned EA" is a test choice so that EA+1 is obviously the XO, not an alignment artifact. The repair does not require a 4- or 8-aligned EA. A base that is not a multiple of 8 still produces an unaligned `ld`/`std`, and DS||00 only forces the displacement to a multiple of 4.

**(g) Disassembler.** File it. P10 is READ: `unassemble` calls `cpu_disassemble_instr` with `running=0` (`debugger_cmds.c:1928`) and returns before the preview read (`cpu_ppc.c:1535-1536`). The trace calls it with `running=1` (`cpu_dyntrans.c:295-296`) and previews through `memory_rw(..., NO_EXCEPTIONS)`. Neither writes a GPR. The fold is real (`cpu_ppc.c:1495`, `1498`, `1517`), and the witness census shows it.

R17 does not admit it: the census oracle would change, which is an oracle correction (R16) with its own red-before-edit, and there is no guest effect. The detector's contract says the census confirms opcode, RT, RA, and the raw low halfword under the disassembler's current text (`ld`/`std` plus the raw halfword). It does not attest the architectural form.

**(h)** CHANGELOG block, a `/* #571 */` tag on each edited decoder line, and the ledger witness term are the usual landing record. Three design points none of the three texts spelled out: both consumers of `imm` (b); `lwa` with `zero` left at 1 is a fourth null the sign-extended oracle kills, and size 3 with `zero` 0 is a host exit; under `step`, `bad:` does not disassemble.

