## Q3

**(a)** The witness file stays byte-identical. The detector is a new `regress/ppc_dsform_probe.py`. Sessions, in order: the load session (C1, C4, C8, C21, `li` last); the store session (C13, C15, then `dump`); five stopping sessions (C12, C19, C20, C6, C17), each `li` then one word; the bogus-TYPE control. No 32-bit session.

Header of the detector, not of the frozen witness: CONTRACT claims the five forms, the three illegal XOs, and the two RA=0 stops. NOT CLAIMED keeps indexed forms, translation, MSR SF, page-crossing, RA=RT, the 32-bit illegality (including the post-fix delta), alignment, and the disassembler's mnemonics. SYMPTOM is the ten rows above. The census sentence stays an observation about raw halfwords.

**(b)** Gate 13, `regress/gate_ppc.sh`, immediately before `need_file` of the NetBSD image (line 203), next to the `rldicl` block. Same machine as that block: `-E bareppc -M 64`. The skips that can swallow it are `need_exec` and a missing `python3`, which already skip the other register probes. Placing it after the image turns a red detector into a skip. Gate 14 is the wrong home: its earlier skips are ARM and m88k prerequisites, and a PPC probe there does not run when those are missing.

**(c)** READ in the working-tree checker (this file differs from HEAD; the `#570` manifest text matches the brief's HEAD quote, shifted by about fifteen lines). `domain()` is `git ls-files -- *.py`. An unlisted tracked `.py` is UNCLASSIFIED. A witness that a gate invokes is GATED WITNESS. So: one `CLASS_DETECTOR` line for `ppc_dsform_probe.py`, naming gate 13 and the pin, and one `CLASS_WITNESS` line for `artifacts/ppcdsformxo/witness_20261024/ppc_dsform_probe.py` once that file is committed. `ppc_dsform_dummy.bin` is not `.py`. Kill-table drivers and frozen copies stay `*.py.txt` / `*.sh.txt`. A `*_probe.py` classified as `tool` is MISFILED (`check_probe_wiring.py:4203-4206`).

**(d)** Score it the way gate 14 scores `#570`: sentinel present and exactly once, `RESULT` parsed with both halves pinned, `CONTROL=OK` once, `SYMPTOM=ABSENT` once, every row by name with `count "^$v  .*ok$"` = 1, emitted / green / red pinned, red = 0. Ignore the exit status (`|| true`); this probe exits 1 when any row is red. Add one pin on `PPC_DSFORM_RED=none` exactly once, the same lesson as the `rldicl` block's single SCORE line. Each stopping session's `live` row is already in the name list. The `%-16s` column and the 15-character assert are already in the witness and have to stay.

**(e)** `probe:regress/artifacts/ppcdsformxo/witness_20261024/ppc_dsform_probe.py` is the right pointer to the load witness that exists. It is not a store measurement. The ledger term should not be edited to imply the store half until the base-binary run of the detector exists.

**(f)** READ. `rlwimi`, `rldimi`, and `rldicl` do not execute this arm (`li` is opcode 14, a different case; the rotates are 21/23/30). `ppc_rounding_probe.py` does: `lfs`/`lfsu`/`lfdu`/`stfs`/`stfsu`/`stfdu`/`stfd` and `stw`, all through this arm, behind the NetBSD image. Re-run it. It will not catch a blanket mask, because those displacements are 0 mod 4. C21 is that catch. I found no other top-level PPC probe that places a D-form load or store.

## Q4

The disassembler fold is real, guest-invisible, and already shown by the witness census (`ld r5,-95(r21)`, `ld r6,10(r22)`). File it at low severity with that witness. No new run. Out of this round, as Q1(g).

`ldx`, `ldux`, `lwax`, and `lwaux` are real and out of scope. READ: `opcodes_ppc.h` has `PPC_31_STDX` (149) and `PPC_31_STDUX` (181), and the indexed arm implements them with no width test. There is no define for XO 21, 53, 341, or 373, so those words hit opcode 31's `default: goto bad` on every CPU, including PPC970. A 64-bit guest's indexed doubleword load or algebraic word load stops the emulator; the matching store is implemented. No hit for those names in `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`. I did not open `ledger.json`. R17: different site, not witnessed here, and the oracle would be an indexed EA. Its own round if it is filed. The class is an unimplemented instruction (CPU stop), not a misdirected store.

Also guest-invisible and not this round: the disassembler has no case for `LFDU`, `LFSU`, `STFDU`, or `STFSU` (no matches in `cpu_ppc.c`). Those opcodes execute since `#310` and disassemble as an unimplemented primary opcode.


